U:RDoc::NormalClass[iI"ERB:ET@I"Object;To:RDoc::Markup::Document:@parts[o;;[åo:RDoc::Markup::Paragraph;[I"©Class *ERB* (the name stands for <b>Embedded Ruby</b>)
is an easy-to-use, but also very powerful, {template processor}[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_processor].;To:RDoc::Markup::Heading:@leveli:
@textI"
Usage;To;	;[I"oBefore you can use \ERB, you must first require it
(examples on this page assume that this has been done):;To:RDoc::Markup::Verbatim;[I"require 'erb'
;T:@format0o;
;i;I"In Brief;To;	;[I"Here's how \ERB works:;To:RDoc::Markup::List:
@type:BULLET:@items[o:RDoc::Markup::ListItem:@label0;[o;	;[I"`You can create a _template_: a plain-text string that includes specially formatted _tags_..;To;;0;[o;	;[I"9You can create an \ERB object to store the template.;To;;0;[o;	;[I"AYou can call instance method ERB#result to get the _result_.;To;	;[I"'\ERB supports tags of three kinds:;To;;;;[o;;0;[
o;	;[I"‘{Expression tags}[rdoc-ref:ERB@Expression+Tags]:
 each begins with <code>'<%='</code>, ends with <code>'%>'</code>; contains a Ruby expression;
 in the result, the value of the expression replaces the entire tag:;To;;[	I"7template = 'The magic word is <%= magic_word %>.'
;FI"erb = ERB.new(template)
;FI"magic_word = 'xyzzy'
;FI"9erb.result(binding) # => "The magic word is xyzzy."
;F;0o;	;[I"®The above call to #result passes argument <code>binding</code>,
which contains the binding of variable <code>magic_word</code> to its string value <code>'xyzzy'</code>.;Fo;	;[I"The below call to #result need not pass a binding,
because its expression <code>Date::DAYNAMES</code> is globally defined.;Fo;;[I"`ERB.new('Today is <%= Date::DAYNAMES[Date.today.wday] %>.').result # => "Today is Monday."
;F;0o;;0;[o;	;[I"ì{Execution tags}[rdoc-ref:ERB@Execution+Tags]:
 each begins with <code>'<%'</code>, ends with <code>'%>'</code>; contains Ruby code to be executed:;To;;[I"< template = '<% File.write("t.txt", "Some stuff.") %>'
;FI" ERB.new(template).result
;FI", File.read('t.txt') # => "Some stuff."
;F;0o;;0;[o;	;[I"Ø{Comment tags}[rdoc-ref:ERB@Comment+Tags]:
 each begins with <code>'<%#'</code>, ends with <code>'%>'</code>; contains comment text;
 in the result, the entire tag is omitted.;To;;[I"] template = 'Some stuff;<%# Note to self: figure out what the stuff is. %> more stuff.'
;FI"> ERB.new(template).result # => "Some stuff; more stuff."
;F;0o;
;i;I"Some Simple Examples;To;	;[I"/Here's a simple example of \ERB in action:;To;;[I"{template = 'The time is <%= Time.now %>.'
erb = ERB.new(template)
erb.result
# => "The time is 2025-09-09 10:49:26 -0500."
;T;0o;	;[I"Details:;To;;:NUMBER;[o;;0;[o;	;[I"—A plain-text string is assigned to variable <code>template</code>.
 Its embedded {expression tag}[rdoc-ref:ERB@Expression+Tags] <code>'<%= Time.now %>'</code> includes a Ruby expression, <code>Time.now</code>.;To;;0;[o;	;[I"WThe string is put into a new \ERB object, and stored in variable <code>erb</code>.;To;;0;[o;	;[I"óMethod call <code>erb.result</code> generates a string that contains the run-time value of <code>Time.now</code>,
 as computed at the time of the call.;To;	;[I"$The
\ERB object may be re-used:;To;;[I">erb.result
# => "The time is 2025-09-09 10:49:33 -0500."
;T;0o;	;[I"Another example:;To;;[I"ütemplate = 'The magic word is <%= magic_word %>.'
erb = ERB.new(template)
magic_word = 'abracadabra'
erb.result(binding)
# => "The magic word is abracadabra."
;T;0o;	;[I"Details:;To;;;;[	o;;0;[o;	;[I"€As before, a plain-text string is assigned to variable <code>template</code>.
 Its embedded {expression tag}[rdoc-ref:ERB@Expression+Tags] <code>'<%= magic_word %>'</code> has a variable _name_, <code>magic_word</code>.;To;;0;[o;	;[I"¨The string is put into a new \ERB object, and stored in variable <code>erb</code>;
 note that <code>magic_word</code> need not be defined before the \ERB object is created.;To;;0;[o;	;[I"a<code>magic_word = 'abracadabra'</code> assigns a value to variable <code>magic_word</code>.;To;;0;[o;	;[I"{Method call <code>erb.result(binding)</code> generates a string
 that contains the _value_ of <code>magic_word</code>.;To;	;[I"/As before, the \ERB object may be re-used:;To;;[I"Nmagic_word = 'xyzzy'
erb.result(binding)
# => "The magic word is xyzzy."
;T;0o;
;i;I"Bindings;To;	;[I"•A call to method #result, which produces the formatted result string,
requires a {Binding object}[https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/master/Binding.html] as its argument.;To;	;[I"qThe binding object provides the bindings for expressions in {expression tags}[rdoc-ref:ERB@Expression+Tags].;To;	;[I":There are three ways to provide the required binding:;To;;;;[o;;0;[o;	;[I"5{Default binding}[rdoc-ref:ERB@Default+Binding].;To;;0;[o;	;[I"1{Local binding}[rdoc-ref:ERB@Local+Binding].;To;;0;[o;	;[I"8{Augmented binding}[rdoc-ref:ERB@Augmented+Binding];To;
;i;I"Default Binding;To;	;[I"˙When you pass no <code>binding</code> argument to method #result,
the method uses its default binding: the one returned by method #new_toplevel.
This binding has the bindings defined by Ruby itself,
which are those for Ruby's constants and variables.;To;	;[I"‡That binding is sufficient for an expression tag that refers only to Ruby's constants and variables;
these expression tags refer only to Ruby's global constant <code>RUBY_COPYRIGHT</code> and global variable <code>$0</code>:;To;;[I"˚template = <<TEMPLATE
The Ruby copyright is <%= RUBY_COPYRIGHT.inspect %>.
The current process is <%= $0 %>.
TEMPLATE
puts ERB.new(template).result
The Ruby copyright is "ruby - Copyright (C) 1993-2025 Yukihiro Matsumoto".
The current process is irb.
;T;0o;	;[I"_(The current process is <code>irb</code> because that's where we're doing these examples!);To;
;i;I"Local Binding;To;	;[I"|The default binding is _not_ sufficient for an expression
that refers to a a constant or variable that is not defined there:;To;;[I"<Foo = 1 # Defines local constant Foo.
foo = 2 # Defines local variable foo.
template = <<TEMPLATE
The current value of constant Foo is <%= Foo %>.
The current value of variable foo is <%= foo %>.
The Ruby copyright is <%= RUBY_COPYRIGHT.inspect %>.
The current process is <%= $0 %>.
TEMPLATE
erb = ERB.new(template)
;T;0o;	;[I"¶This call below raises <code>NameError</code> because although <code>Foo</code> and <code>foo</code> are defined locally,
they are not defined in the default binding:;To;;[I"$erb.result # Raises NameError.
;T;0o;	;[I"pTo make the locally-defined constants and variables available,
you can call #result with the local binding:;To;;[I"–puts erb.result(binding)
The current value of constant Foo is 1.
The current value of variable foo is 2.
The Ruby copyright is "ruby - Copyright (C) 1993-2025 Yukihiro Matsumoto".
The current process is irb.
;T;0o;
;i;I"Augmented Binding;To;	;[I"ÔAnother way to make variable bindings (but not constant bindings) available
is to use method #result_with_hash(hash);
the passed hash has name/value pairs that are to be used to define and assign variables
in a copy of the default binding:;To;;[I"template = <<TEMPLATE
The current value of variable bar is <%= bar %>.
The current value of variable baz is <%= baz %>.
The Ruby copyright is <%= RUBY_COPYRIGHT.inspect %>.
The current process is <%= $0 %>.
TEMPLATE
erb = ERB.new(template)
;T;0o;	;[I"£Both of these calls raise <code>NameError</code>, because <code>bar</code> and <code>baz</code>
are not defined in either the default binding or the local binding.;To;;[I"_puts erb.result          # Raises NameError.
puts erb.result(binding) # Raises NameError.
;T;0o;	;[I"ÜThis call passes a hash that causes <code>bar</code> and <code>baz</code> to be defined
in a new binding (derived from #new_toplevel):;To;;[I"Ôhash = {bar: 3, baz: 4}
puts erb.result_with_hash(hash)
The current value of variable bar is 3.
The current value of variable baz is 4.
The Ruby copyright is "ruby - Copyright (C) 1993-2025 Yukihiro Matsumoto".
The current process is irb.
;T;0o;
;i;I"	Tags;To;	;[I"RThe examples above use expression tags.
These are the tags available in \ERB:;To;;;;[o;;0;[o;	;[I"Ø{Expression tag}[rdoc-ref:ERB@Expression+Tags]: the tag contains a Ruby expression;
 in the result, the entire tag is to be replaced with the run-time value of the expression.;To;;0;[o;	;[I"ü{Execution tag}[rdoc-ref:ERB@Execution+Tags]: the tag contains Ruby code;
 in the result, the entire tag is to be replaced with the run-time value of the code.;To;;0;[o;	;[I"~{Comment tag}[rdoc-ref:ERB@Comment+Tags]: the tag contains comment code;
 in the result, the entire tag is to be omitted.;To;
;i;I"Expression Tags;To;	;[I"TYou can embed a Ruby expression in a template using an <em>expression tag</em>.;To;	;[I"eIts syntax is <code><%= _expression_ %></code>,
where _expression_ is any valid Ruby expression.;To;	;[I"ÖWhen you call method #result,
the method evaluates the expression and replaces the entire expression tag with the expression's value:;To;;[I"4ERB.new('Today is <%= Date::DAYNAMES[Date.today.wday] %>.').result
# => "Today is Monday."
ERB.new('Tomorrow will be <%= Date::DAYNAMES[Date.today.wday + 1] %>.').result
# => "Tomorrow will be Tuesday."
ERB.new('Yesterday was <%= Date::DAYNAMES[Date.today.wday - 1] %>.').result
# => "Yesterday was Sunday."
;T;0o;	;[I"áNote that whitespace before and after the expression
is allowed but not required,
and that such whitespace is stripped from the result.;To;;[I"˘ERB.new('My appointment is on <%=Date::DAYNAMES[Date.today.wday + 2]%>.').result
# => "My appointment is on Wednesday."
ERB.new('My appointment is on <%=     Date::DAYNAMES[Date.today.wday + 2]    %>.').result
# => "My appointment is on Wednesday."
;T;0o;
;i;I"Execution Tags;To;	;[I"TYou can embed Ruby executable code in template using an <em>execution tag</em>.;To;	;[I"RIts syntax is <code><% _code_ %></code>,
where _code_ is any valid Ruby code.;To;	;[I"ÉWhen you call method #result,
the method executes the code and removes the entire execution tag
(generating no text in the result):;To;;[I"FERB.new('foo <% Dir.chdir("C:/") %> bar').result # => "foo  bar"
;T;0o;	;[I"?Whitespace before and after the embedded code is optional:;To;;[I"FERB.new('foo <%Dir.chdir("C:/")%> bar').result   # => "foo  bar"
;T;0o;	;[I"âYou can interleave text with execution tags to form a control structure
such as a conditional, a loop, or a <code>case</code> statements.;To;	;[I"Conditional:;To;;[I"˛template = <<TEMPLATE
<% if verbosity %>
An error has occurred.
<% else %>
Oops!
<% end %>
TEMPLATE
erb = ERB.new(template)
verbosity = true
erb.result(binding)
# => "\nAn error has occurred.\n\n"
verbosity = false
erb.result(binding)
# => "\nOops!\n\n"
;T;0o;	;[I"GNote that the interleaved text may itself contain expression tags:;To;	;[I"
Loop:;To;;[I"∏template = <<TEMPLATE
<% Date::ABBR_DAYNAMES.each do |dayname| %>
<%= dayname %>
<% end %>
TEMPLATE
ERB.new(template).result
# => "\nSun\n\nMon\n\nTue\n\nWed\n\nThu\n\nFri\n\nSat\n\n"
;T;0o;	;[I"ÑOther, non-control, lines of Ruby code may be interleaved with the text,
and the Ruby code may itself contain regular Ruby comments:;To;;[I"˚template = <<TEMPLATE
<% 3.times do %>
<%= Time.now %>
<% sleep(1) # Let's make the times different. %>
<% end %>
TEMPLATE
ERB.new(template).result
# => "\n2025-09-09 11:36:02 -0500\n\n\n2025-09-09 11:36:03 -0500\n\n\n2025-09-09 11:36:04 -0500\n\n\n"
;T;0o;	;[I"?The execution tag may also contain multiple lines of code:;To;;[I"Ètemplate = <<TEMPLATE
<%
  (0..2).each do |i|
    (0..2).each do |j|
%>
* <%=i%>,<%=j%>
<%
    end
  end
%>
TEMPLATE
ERB.new(template).result
# => "\n* 0,0\n\n* 0,1\n\n* 0,2\n\n* 1,0\n\n* 1,1\n\n* 1,2\n\n* 2,0\n\n* 2,1\n\n* 2,2\n\n"
;T;0o;
;i	;I"(Shorthand Format for Execution Tags;To;	;[I"ÃYou can use keyword argument <code>trim_mode: '%'</code> to enable a shorthand format for execution tags;
this example uses the shorthand format <code>% _code_</code> instead of <code><% _code_ %></code>:;To;;[I"Wtemplate = <<TEMPLATE
% priorities.each do |priority|
  * <%= priority %>
% end
TEMPLATE
erb = ERB.new(template, trim_mode: '%')
priorities = [ 'Run Ruby Quiz',
               'Document Modules',
               'Answer Questions on Ruby Talk' ]
puts erb.result(binding)
  * Run Ruby Quiz
  * Document Modules
  * Answer Questions on Ruby Talk
;T;0o;	;[I"áNote that in the shorthand format, the character <code>'%'</code> must be the first character in the code line
(no leading whitespace).;To;
;i	;I"%Suppressing Unwanted Blank Lines;To;	;[I"`With keyword argument <code>trim_mode</code> not given,
all blank lines go into the result:;To;;[I"ütemplate = <<TEMPLATE
<% if true %>
<%= RUBY_VERSION %>
<% end %>
TEMPLATE
ERB.new(template).result.lines.each {|line| puts line.inspect }
"\n"
"3.4.5\n"
"\n"
;T;0o;	;[I"ïYou can give <code>trim_mode: '-'</code>, you can suppress each blank line
whose source line ends with <code>-%></code> (instead of <code>%></code>):;To;;[I"ßtemplate = <<TEMPLATE
<% if true -%>
<%= RUBY_VERSION %>
<% end -%>
TEMPLATE
ERB.new(template, trim_mode: '-').result.lines.each {|line| puts line.inspect }
"3.4.5\n"
;T;0o;	;[I"hIt is an error to use the trailing <code>'-%>'</code> notation without <code>trim_mode: '-'</code>:;To;;[I"[ERB.new(template).result.lines.each {|line| puts line.inspect } # Raises SyntaxError.
;T;0o;
;i	;I""Suppressing Unwanted Newlines;To;	;[I"Consider this template:;To;;[I"ztemplate = <<TEMPLATE
<% RUBY_VERSION %>
<%= RUBY_VERSION %>
foo <% RUBY_VERSION %>
foo <%= RUBY_VERSION %>
TEMPLATE
;T;0o;	;[I"]With keyword argument <code>trim_mode</code> not given, all newlines go into the result:;To;;[I"kERB.new(template).result.lines.each {|line| puts line.inspect }
"\n"
"3.4.5\n"
"foo \n"
"foo 3.4.5\n"
;T;0o;	;[I"óYou can give <code>trim_mode: '>'</code> to suppress the trailing newline
for each line that ends with <code>'%>'</code> (regardless of its beginning):;To;;[I"jERB.new(template, trim_mode: '>').result.lines.each {|line| puts line.inspect }
"3.4.5foo foo 3.4.5"
;T;0o;	;[I"°You can give <code>trim_mode: '<>'</code> to suppress the trailing newline
for each line that both begins with <code>'<%'</code> and ends with <code>'%>'</code>:;To;;[I"rERB.new(template, trim_mode: '<>').result.lines.each {|line| puts line.inspect }
"3.4.5foo \n"
"foo 3.4.5\n"
;T;0o;
;i	;I"Combining Trim Modes;To;	;[I"(You can combine certain trim modes:;To;;;;[o;;0;[o;	;[I"a<code>'%-'</code>: Enable shorthand and omit each blank line ending with <code>'-%>'</code>.;To;;0;[o;	;[I"f<code>'%>'</code>: Enable shorthand and omit newline for each line ending with <code>'%>'</code>.;To;;0;[o;	;[I"Ü<code>'%<>'</code>: Enable shorthand and omit newline for each line starting with <code>'<%'</code> and ending with <code>'%>'</code>.;To;
;i;I"Comment Tags;To;	;[I"ñYou can embed a comment in a template using a <em>comment tag</em>;
its syntax is <code><%# _text_ %></code>,
where _text_ is the text of the comment.;To;	;[I"hWhen you call method #result,
it removes the entire comment tag
(generating no text in the result).;To;	;[I"Example:;To;;[I"ètemplate = 'Some stuff;<%# Note to self: figure out what the stuff is. %> more stuff.'
ERB.new(template).result # => "Some stuff; more stuff."
;T;0o;	;[I"7A comment tag may appear anywhere in the template.;To;	;[I"\Note that the beginning of the tag must be <code>'<%#'</code>, not <code>'<% #'</code>.;To;	;[I"øIn this example, the tag begins with <code>'<% #'</code>, and so is an execution tag, not a comment tag;
the cited code consists entirely of a Ruby-style comment (which is of course ignored):;To;;[I"uERB.new('Some stuff;<% # Note to self: figure out what the stuff is. %> more stuff.').result
# => "Some stuff;"
;T;0o;
;i;I"Encodings;To;	;[I"æAn \ERB object has an {encoding}[https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/master/Encoding.html],
which is by default the encoding of the template string;
the result string will also have that encoding.;To;;[I"»template = <<TEMPLATE
<%# Comment. %>
TEMPLATE
erb = ERB.new(template)
template.encoding   # => #<Encoding:UTF-8>
erb.encoding        # => #<Encoding:UTF-8>
erb.result.encoding # => #<Encoding:UTF-8>
;T;0o;	;[I"∑You can specify a different encoding by adding a {magic comment}[https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/master/syntax/comments_rdoc.html#label-Magic+Comments]
at the top of the given template:;To;;[I"‡template = <<TEMPLATE
<%#-*- coding: Big5 -*-%>
<%# Comment. %>
TEMPLATE
erb = ERB.new(template)
template.encoding   # => #<Encoding:UTF-8>
erb.encoding        # => #<Encoding:Big5>
erb.result.encoding # => #<Encoding:Big5>
;T;0o;
;i;I"Error Reporting;To;	;[I"2Consider this template (containing an error):;To;;[I"8template = '<%= nosuch %>'
erb = ERB.new(template)
;T;0o;	;[I"•When \ERB reports an error,
it includes a file name (if available) and a line number;
the file name comes from method #filename, the line number from method #lineno.;To;	;[I"™Initially, those values are <code>nil</code> and <code>0</code>, respectively;
these initial values are reported as <code>'(erb)'</code> and <code>1</code>, respectively:;To;;[I"ãerb.filename # => nil
erb.lineno   # => 0
erb.result
(erb):1:in '<main>': undefined local variable or method 'nosuch' for main (NameError)
;T;0o;	;[I"kYou can use methods #filename= and #lineno= to assign values
that are more meaningful in your context:;To;;[I"ãerb.filename = 't.txt'
erb.lineno = 555
erb.result
t.txt:556:in '<main>': undefined local variable or method 'nosuch' for main (NameError)
;T;0o;	;[I"6You can use method #location= to set both values:;To;;[I"Çerb.location = ['u.txt', 999]
erb.result
u.txt:1000:in '<main>': undefined local variable or method 'nosuch' for main (NameError)
;T;0o;
;i;I""Plain Text with Embedded Ruby;To;	;[I"Here's a plain-text template;
it uses the literal notation <code>'%q{ ... }'</code> to define the template
(see {%q literals}[https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/master/syntax/literals_rdoc.html#label-25q-3A+Non-Interpolable+String+Literals]);
this avoids problems with backslashes.;To;;[I"Õtemplate = %q{
From:  James Edward Gray II <james@grayproductions.net>
To:  <%= to %>
Subject:  Addressing Needs

<%= to[/\w+/] %>:

Just wanted to send a quick note assuring that your needs are being
addressed.

I want you to know that my team will keep working on the issues,
especially:

<%# ignore numerous minor requests -- focus on priorities %>
% priorities.each do |priority|
  * <%= priority %>
% end

Thanks for your patience.

James Edward Gray II
}
;T;0o;	;[I""The template will need these:;To;;[I"Æto = 'Community Spokesman <spokesman@ruby_community.org>'
priorities = [ 'Run Ruby Quiz',
